WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.960
An AI agent is just another system
process, but with a unique job.

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.100
It takes actions on your behalf.

3
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.470
In this unit, we use a micro-LLM.

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.450
It has exactly 175,000 parameters, and its
only task is to translate an English or

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.460
Spanish request into a specific tool call.

6
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.010
But a language model is a statistical
engine, not a deterministic program.

7
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.590
It can easily hallucinate a command or be
manipulated by a hidden instruction in a

8
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.220
document it reads, an attack
known as prompt injection.

9
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.390
Because the output is simply untrusted
text, the operating system cannot assume the

10
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.980
agent's decisions are safe.

11
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.520
To test this, we are going to run a
penetration test on our own system.

12
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.930
We start by feeding the agent a
deliberately malicious prompt.

13
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:57.380
We ask it to read the highly sensitive
password file at /etc/passwd.

14
00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.720
The model does its job perfectly.

15
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.320
It translates the malicious intent and
generates the exact tool call requested,

16
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.880
targeting that secure file.

17
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.440
Even though the agent was started by
the user, it is an independent entity.

18
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.900
The system does not automatically grant
the user's full operational trust.

19
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.940
This brings us to the core
rule of our architecture.

20
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.550
The AI model proposes an action, but the
operating system disposes, deciding if that

21
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.500
action is actually allowed to run.

22
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.190
That untrusted text travels over a Unix
socket with 0600 permissions straight into

23
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.830
the agentd daemon. This central manager
uses a thread pool to handle concurrent

24
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.980
requests. Instead of asking the agent,
agentd asks the kernel directly.

25
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.380
Using SO_PEERCRED, it retrieves
the client's UID and PID.

26
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:50.280
Every single decision and action is then
permanently recorded into an audit log.

27
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.930
Before any action is even considered, the
system mandates that the kernel verify the

28
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.940
caller's identity, leaving
a permanent paper trail.

29
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.700
Normally, the request hits
a strict policy layer next.

30
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.210
The daemon parses the text, checks it
against a closed catalog of tools, and forces

31
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.440
all file paths to stay inside
the agent's safe workspace.

32
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.410
If the action is destructive, the policy
pauses everything to ask the user for

33
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.970
explicit consent. But to test the true
resilience of our operating system, we are

34
00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.340
going to intentionally
disable this policy engine.

35
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.130
With the application-level gatekeeper turned
off, our malicious request to read the

36
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.200
password file slips right through,
moving freely toward execution.

37
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.090
Removing this safety net illustrates why
relying on just one layer of application

38
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.020
security leaves a system
highly vulnerable.

39
00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.140
The request reaches the
enforcer program, agent_exec.

40
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.490
It prepares to run the tool by spawning
a new process using the fork and execve

41
00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:54.530
system calls. It immediately applies strict
memory and file descriptor limits using

42
00:02:54.580 --> 00:03:00.160
setrlimit and sets an alarm to guarantee
the untrusted code cannot run endlessly.

43
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.690
Now, this highly restricted process is
isolated, primed, and ready to execute the

44
00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.320
attack on our system file.

45
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.450
While these resource limits protect the
machine from crashing, they do absolutely

46
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.620
nothing to stop the process from
opening and reading secure data.

47
00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.620
The moment of truth arrives.

48
00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:22.530
The isolated rogue process finally attempts
to execute the command to open /etc/passwd.

49
00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:28.290
To do this, the process, which lives
up in user mode, must cross the

50
00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.780
privilege boundary down into
kernel mode to access a file.

51
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.900
Here, the kernel makes
a decisive intervention.

52
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:40.330
Because agent_exec previously applied a
Landlock sandbox, the kernel flatly denies

53
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.300
the access request. This is the
defining feature of Landlock.

54
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:49.010
Once a process restricts itself, that sandbox
is permanently inherited and cannot be

55
00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.860
undone, even if the
application's policy fails.

56
00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.660
True defense in depth means assuming every
application layer will eventually break.

57
00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:01.600
When they do, the kernel sandbox stands
as the ultimate, unbypassable authority.

58
00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:07.310
Let's review. First, which socket option
provides the daemon with the exact identity

59
00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.600
of the client? It is SO_PEERCRED.

60
00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.920
Second, which Linux primitive acts as the
final kernel sandbox to stop the agent?

61
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.890
That is Landlock. Now open animations
A9.2 and A9.4 in the course hub

62
00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.740
and run lab TD8 with its AI challenge.
